Introducing vector
The standard C++ libraries provide various helpful datatypes, e.g. std::vector

Solution main_vector1.cpp
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

int main() {
  std::vector&lt;double&gt; v(5);
  v.reserve(10);
  v[0] = 1.0;
  v[2] = 1.0;
  std::cout &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  std::cout &lt;&lt; v[0] &lt;&lt; " " &lt;&lt; v[1] &lt;&lt; " " &lt;&lt; v[2] &lt;&lt; std::endl;

  return 0;
}


5
1 0 1



Note that you need to put an std:: in front of the vector (and cout, endl). This is a namespace which we will cover later.
indexing starts at \(0\) in C++!
some useful functions size, reserve, resize

Conditionals
C/C++ comes with the basic conditionals: if, else if, else
if ( boolean == true ){
    // statement executed if the condition boolean == true is valid
} else if (boolean1 == true){
    // statement executed if the condition boolean == true is not valid but boolean1 == true
} else{
    // statement executed if both conditions are false
}
New variables defined inside these environments are unknown to the global scope.

Loops
C/C++ comes with the basic loops: for, while, do while
for ( long i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++ ){
   // statement
}

while ( boolean == true ){
   // statement
}

do {
  // statement
} while( boolean == true )
To exit a loop early we can use break and continue to jup along

Range based loops
The range based of often also called for each loop is available in C++ as well
std::vector&lt;int&gt; numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

// Range-based for loop
for (int num : numbers) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; num &lt;&lt; " ";
}

// Range-based for loop with auto key word
for (auto num : numbers) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; num &lt;&lt; " ";
}
